+//
+// llama_residency_init solves ONE model against a RAM budget. In a three-tier
+// process that budget is shared, so duo has to hand it the two cross-terms that
+// say how much of --max-ram-mib the other tiers have already spent, and withhold
+// its own context in `reserve_mib`. The derivation, per tier:
+//
+//   mlock_front_mib    every MLOCK tier's LOCK RANGE. Measured with
+//                      llama_model_mlock_bytes() once that tier is loaded (the
+//                      front always is: main loads it before the loader thread
+//                      exists), and header-inclusive because the loader grows the
+//                      lock from the mapping base -- see the POL_MLOCK comment in
+//                      load_duo_model. A refused lock reads back as 0, which is
+//                      honest for "locked" but wrong for "resident", so the tensor
+//                      span is used as the floor in that case.
+//   resident_other_mib every OTHER tier's context (KV + recurrent state), which is
+//                      allocated once and never freed, PLUS the weights of tiers
+//                      that are unconditionally resident (POL_RESIDENT). A tier
+//                      that is STREAMED contributes only its context: while THIS
+//                      tier is the active one, that tier has been suspended, i.e.
+//                      bulk-evicted including its pins (see duo_switch_to).
+//   reserve_mib        this tier's own context + --stream-reserve-mib for the
+//                      compute buffers, logits and slack.
+//
+// Every term is itemized in the [ledger] block below rather than folded into one
+// number, because the interesting ones are the surprising ones: a tier loaded with
+// a checkpoint sequence has its n_ctx DOUBLED (load_duo_model), and every hybrid
+// tier gets a checkpoint sequence whether or not the mode asked for one, so the
+// mid tier's KV line is twice the size --ctx-expert suggests.
